meta_title: "Free QR Code Generator: Link, Wi-Fi, UPI, Phone, Email, PNG and SVG"
meta_description: "Make a QR code for a link, text, Wi-Fi login, phone number, email or a UPI payment. Choose the error correction and size, then download a PNG or SVG. The codes never expire and nothing is uploaded."
meta_keywords: "qr code generator, free qr code generator, wifi qr code, upi qr code generator, qr code for link, qr code svg, qr code png, static qr code"
noIconFont: true
faqs:
  - q: "Do these QR codes expire?"
    a: "No. The link, text or login is stored inside the pattern itself, so the code works for as long as the printed copy can be scanned. Some sites sell dynamic codes that point to their own short link and stop working when you stop paying. The codes made here do not go through any server."
  - q: "Which error correction level should I pick?"
    a: "Medium suits most uses. Higher levels add more spare data, so a code that is scratched, partly covered or printed with a logo on top can still be read, but the pattern gets denser. Low is fine for a code shown on a screen. High is the choice for a code printed outdoors or on packaging."
  - q: "How does a UPI QR code work?"
    a: "It holds a UPI payment link with your UPI ID, your name and, if you enter one, an amount. Any UPI app that scans it opens a payment screen with those details filled in. The payer still checks the name and confirms with their UPI PIN. This page only writes the link into a picture and never handles money."
  - q: "Can a Wi-Fi QR code connect a phone without typing the password?"
    a: "Yes. The camera apps on recent Android phones and iPhones read the Wi-Fi format and offer to join the network. Anyone who can see the code can also join, so print it where only your guests will see it, and make a new code if you change the password."
  - q: "PNG or SVG?"
    a: "PNG is a picture of fixed size that works everywhere, from WhatsApp to a Word file. SVG is a drawing that stays sharp at any size, so use it for posters, banners and anything sent to a printer."
  - q: "Is anything I type sent to a server?"
    a: "No. The code is drawn by your browser from what you type. Wi-Fi passwords, UPI IDs and phone numbers stay on your device and are not saved after you close the page."
---

